# ⏱️ Time & Space Complexity — DSA Interview Notes

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🔵 1. What is Time Complexity?

Time Complexity tells us how the running time/work of an algorithm grows when the input size `n` increases.

### 🎯 Interview Answer

"Time complexity tells us how the running time of an algorithm grows as the input size increases."

### 💡 Real-Life Example

If you have 10 books, finding a particular book may be easy.

If you have 10,000 books, the amount of work may increase.

Time complexity helps us understand how an algorithm behaves when the input becomes large.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🟢 2. What is Space Complexity?

Space Complexity tells us how much extra memory an algorithm needs while running.

### 🎯 Interview Answer

"Space complexity is the amount of extra memory an algorithm needs while running."

### 🧠 Easy Trick

⏱️ Time → How much work?

💾 Space → How much memory?

### 💡 Real-Life Example

Cooking:

⏱️ Time → How long does cooking take?

💾 Space → How much counter/storage space is needed?

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🟡 3. O(1) — Constant Time

The amount of work stays almost the same even when `n` increases.

### 💻 Example

int x = arr[5];

Accessing an array element using its index takes O(1).

### 🧠 Remember

Fixed work → O(1)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🟠 4. O(n) — Linear Time

The work increases directly with the input size.

### 💻 Example

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

The loop runs `n` times.

Therefore:

O(n)

### 🧠 Remember

One loop that runs `n` times → usually O(n).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🔴 5. O(n²) — Quadratic Time

The work grows approximately as `n × n`.

### 💻 Example

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        System.out.println(i + j);
    }
}

n × n = n²

Therefore:

O(n²)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🟣 6. O(log n) — Logarithmic Time

The input is repeatedly divided or the value repeatedly doubles/halves.

### 💻 Example

int i = 1;

while (i < n) {
    i = i * 2;
}

Values:

1 → 2 → 4 → 8 → 16 → ...

Therefore:

O(log n)

### 🧠 Memory Trick

i = i * 2 → think LOG

i = i / 2 → think LOG

### 🎯 Common Example

Binary Search → O(log n)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🔵 7. O(n log n)

An O(n) operation is repeated O(log n) times.

### Example

O(n) × O(log n)

= O(n log n)

### 🎯 Common Example

Merge Sort → O(n log n)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## ⭐ 8. Separate Loops vs Nested Loops

### ➕ Separate Loops → ADD

O(n) + O(n)

= O(2n)

= O(n)

### ✖️ Nested Loops → MULTIPLY

O(n) × O(n)

= O(n²)

### 🧠 Golden Rule

Separate → ADD ➕

Nested → MULTIPLY ✖️

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🟢 9. Constant Loops

A loop that runs a fixed number of times is O(1).

Example:

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    // work
}

10 is fixed.

So:

O(10) = O(1)

If:

O(10n) → O(n)

O(100n) → O(n)

### 🧠 Remember

Fixed number → Ignore it.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🔥 10. Remove Constants

We normally ignore constant multipliers.

O(2n) → O(n)

O(10n) → O(n)

O(5n²) → O(n²)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## ⭐ 11. Keep the Biggest Term

Example:

O(n² + n)

Keep the fastest-growing term:

O(n²)

Example:

O(n² + n + 10)

= O(n²)

### 🧠 Remember

Keep the term that grows the fastest.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 💾 12. Time and Space Can Be Different

Example:

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    sum += i;
}

⏱️ Time → O(n)

💾 Space → O(1)

Why?

The loop works `n` times, but it does not store `n` new values.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 💾 13. Array Space

int[] arr = new int[n];

The array stores `n` elements.

Therefore:

Space → O(n)

### 🧠 Remember

If memory grows with `n` → O(n).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🏆 14. Complexity Order

For large input, generally:

O(1)
↓
O(log n)
↓
O(n)
↓
O(n log n)
↓
O(n²)
↓
O(n³)
↓
O(2ⁿ)

Smaller growth is generally better for large inputs.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🎯 15. Interview Tips

⭐ Don't decide complexity only by counting loops.

⭐ Check how many times the loop actually runs.

⭐ Separate loops → ADD.

⭐ Nested loops → MULTIPLY.

⭐ `i++` → usually O(n).

⭐ `i = i * 2` → usually O(log n).

⭐ `i = i / 2` → usually O(log n).

⭐ Fixed loop like 10 times → O(1).

⭐ Remove constants.

⭐ Keep the biggest-growing term.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## 🧠 QUICK MEMORY TRICKS

"Direct access" → O(1)

"One full pass" → O(n)

"Keep cutting in half" → O(log n)

"n × log n" → O(n log n)

"n × n" → O(n²)

"Separate" → ADD ➕

"Nested" → MULTIPLY ✖️

